[]E:}\cell\pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\intbl\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa0{\loch
E:\\ on Windows, /mnt/e on WSL/Linux}\cell\pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\intbl\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa0{\loch
explicit blank; rejected if OS label is nonblank}\cell\row\pard \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl252\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Use the same bracketed volume name each time you scan the same drive. For example, use [Backup-1]E:\\Music on Windows or [Backup-1]/mnt/e/Music on WSL/Linux for the same external drive. If no bracketed prefix is supplied, TLO resolves and uses the operating-system volume label when it can. The default labels New Volume and Local Disk are treated as blank rather than as useful unique volume names.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
TLO tracks inventory by visible volume name plus the path below the volume. It ignores and does not store the drive letter or WSL /mnt/<drive> prefix in bootlist.csv or the top SEARCH_PATH log headers. For example, [Backup-1]/mnt/e/Music and [Backup-1]/mnt/n/Music are treated as the same stored root [Backup-1] /Music when they refer to the same labeled volume.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
If no bracketed volume name is supplied, TLO substitutes the operating-system volume label for logging and existing-volume comparison when that label can be resolved. The default Windows-style labels New Volume and Local Disk are treated as blank rather than as useful volume names.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
If an explicit bracketed volume name is supplied, it must match the mounted drive label when that label can be resolved. A mismatch raises an alert and that path is not processed.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
For a labeled drive, TLO no longer asks whether to skip or re-inventory when it sees the same volume again. It applies the path relationship automatically: same path or parent path replaces that scope; child path replaces only entries below that child path; non-overlapping paths on the same volume are added to the same volume log header and appended.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
After a path-scoped re-inventory, TLO prints a concise re-inventory reconciliation line. TLOHome/logs/reinventoryDelta.log contains the full old-to-new audit: each previous bootlist row is labeled UNCHANGED, METADATA_CHANGED, RENAMED, RENAMED_METADATA_CHANGED, SPLIT, MERGED, REGROUPED, CORRUPTION_REMOVED, UNRESOLVED, or MISSING, with old/current Show and path values plus the reason. Current rows with no previous-row match are listed as NEW. The log also retains the overall previous/prepared/Stage 3/corruption/unresolved/final-row counts. summary.log receives the compact counts and disposition totals instead of repeating every per-row detail block. This makes a genuine disappearance stand out as MISSING instead of being hidden by a net row count.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Explicit [] means an explicit blank volume. It is rejected when the mounted drive has a nonblank, non-default operating-system volume label.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
For drives with a blank volume label, or with the default labels New Volume or Local Disk, TLO uses one append-only blank-volume log set. The log header is SEARCH_PATH: [] with no path. There is no skip or re-inventory for blank/default-label drives, so you must avoid or clean up duplicates yourself if the same blank/default-label drive content is scanned multiple times.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
On native Windows, physical-drive grouping calls only the Windows-supplied System32 PowerShell, located through the Windows directory API rather than PATH. If the trusted helper is unavailable, TLO uses its existing visible-volume fallback. WSL keeps its separate interoperability lookup.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa60{\loch
When more than one path is queued, TLO schedules labeled volumes first. Paths on the same named physical drive run serially; different physical drives may run in parallel according to Performance Mode and Max Workers. After labeled work completes, all blank-label paths run serially in drive-letter order.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
A single labeled volume log can list multiple SEARCH_PATH header lines when you add non-overlapping roots from the same drive over time.}
\par \trowd\trqc\ltrrow\trkeep\trpaddft3\trpaddt0\trpaddfl3\trpaddl0\trpaddfb3\trpaddb0\trpaddfr3\trpaddr0\clpadft3\clpadt108\clpadfr3\clpadr108\clcbpat23\cellx8316\pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\intbl\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa20{\loch
Mount aliases: TLO rejects obvious duplicate and overlapping roots after normalization, but symlinks, junctions, case-sensitive aliases, and OS-specific mount aliases can still create duplicates if they point to the same content.}\cell\row\pard \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl252\slmult1\sb0\sa60\loch

\par \pard\plain \s2\rtlch\af12\afs26\ab \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\sl240\slmult1\keep\sb200\sa0\keepn\cf18\f4\fs26\b\dbch\af9\sl240\slmult1\sb180\sa80{\loch
Required Search Path and optional control files}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl252\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Search Path is required for every full inventory run. Enter one direct inventory root or multiple entries separated by semicolons. Each direct entry may use the same optional [Volume] prefix and per-path directives shown below. A Search Path entry that ends in .txt is treated as an inventory-control file; the file may have any filename and uses the former toBeInventoried.txt format, with one inventory root per non-comment line. Blank lines and lines whose first non-whitespace character is # are ignored, and lines beginning with REM are also comments. TLOHome/toBeInventoried.txt is never read automatically; COMPLETE packages continue to include it only as an example/template. To use that template, explicitly enter its fully qualified filename in Search Path.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Windows root-path examples can be entered as E:, E:\\, or H:.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
For WSL/Linux full inventory, Windows drive paths are translated to /mnt/<drive>/... while preserving the full subtree. Commas are ordinary path characters and do not act as Path(s) separators. For example, D:\\B-J\\D\\Dudek, Les is one path entry without requiring quotes and is translated normally under WSL/Linux. Quotes remain accepted if supplied.}
\par \pard\plain \s72\rtlch\af12\afs17\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af8\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\li360\lin360\ri144\rin144\sb40\sa80\ltrpar\cf0\f8\fs17\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af13\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\fi0\li360\lin360\ri144\rin144\sb60\sa120{\loch
REM examples for Windows\line [External-E]E:\line [External-E]E:\\\line [Archive-H]H:\line [Backup-1]E:\\Music\line [Archive-F]F:\\boots --/slam "Bob Dylan"\line [Archive-G]G:\\incoming --/copy H:\\TaggedCopies\line [Archive-I]I:\\new --/copy-delete J:\\TaggedFinished --/slam "Bill Dickens"\line \line REM examples for WSL/Linux\line [Backup-1]/mnt/e/Music\line [Archive-F]/mnt/f/boots --/slam "Bob Dylan"\line [Archive-G]/mnt/g/incoming --/copy /mnt/h/TaggedCopies\line [Archive-I]/mnt/i/new --/copy-delete /mnt/j/TaggedFinished --/slam "Bill Dickens"}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Optional per-path directives can be added after the path. Use --/slam ARTIST_NAME to force an artist, --/copy DESTINATION to copy and tag that path's folders at the destination, or --/copy-delete DESTINATION to copy/move, verify when needed, delete the original, and tag the transferred folders.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
The order of --/slam and the copy directive does not matter, but a single line cannot contain both --/copy and --/copy-delete. Destination folders must already exist and must be fully qualified paths.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Before inventory starts, TLO checks whether each copy destination has enough free space for the source root or roots assigned to it. If a destination is short on space, TLO stops and reports the destination, the affected source path or paths, and how much additional space is needed.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
When this capacity check is needed, the console first prints a Note explaining that TLO is checking source folder sizes before it checks destination capacity. Large roots can take a while to total, similar to opening folder Properties.}
\par \trowd\trqc\ltrrow\trkeep\trpaddft3\trpaddt0\trpaddfl3\trpaddl0\trpaddfb3\trpaddb0\trpaddfr3\trpaddr0\clpadft3\clpadt108\clpadfr3\clpadr108\clcbpat23\cellx8316\pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\intbl\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200{\loch
Volume label behavior: If no bracketed prefix is supplied, TLO uses the operating-system volume label for comparison and logging when available. Use [Volume] only when it matches the mounted drive label. Use [] only when an explicitly blank volume is intended and the mounted drive label is blank or unavailable.}\cell\row\pard \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl252\slmult1\sb0\sa60\loch

\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Windows network shares accept fully qualified UNC paths such as \\\\ONARACS\\Music\\Grateful Dead. When no reliable volume label exists, TLO uses the share-root identity UNC:onaracs/music. Shares must be accessible with the current Windows credentials; Copy Requests can refer to the recorded share identity.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af12\afs32\ab \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\sl240\slmult1\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn\cf17\f4\fs32\b\dbch\af9\sl240\slmult1\sb240\sa120{\loch
9. Running the main GUI}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl252\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Start the Windows GUI with TLOHome/apps/Windows/tlo-main.exe, or the WSL/Linux GUI from TLOHome/apps/Linux. The GUI can also be preloaded with command-line arguments such as --TLOHome, --search-path, --compliant, --as-is-artist-name, --etree-lookup, --setlistfm-lookup, --setlistfm-upgrade, --thorough-setlist-matching, --corrupt-files, --corrupt-folders, --corrupt-folder-threshold, --performance-mode, and --max-workers. The main Inventory and Search windows do not show an editable TLOHome box; the startup value is used for the whole session. In the main Inventory window, the resolved TLOHome appears on the same top row as the application title, immediately to the left of the hamburger menu. The required Search Path field is labeled Path(s). Its entry accepts semicolon-separated direct roots using the same per-entry syntax as toBeInventoried.txt, plus .txt control-file paths. One-line checkbox labels are vertically aligned with their indicators; wrapped checkbox labels keep the indicator aligned with the first text line. There is no separate Slam entry; use --/slam on the applicable Path(s) entry or control-file line. The header and top option rows use minimal vertical spacing.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
On native Windows installations, drag one or more folders and/or .txt control files from File Explorer onto the Path(s) text box. TLO processes the complete Windows drag payload, so every item selected in one drag is retained. Each drag appends its items to the existing Path(s) value in drop order, with semicolons inserted automatically. You can drag one folder, then another, then several folders together; all remain in Path(s). TLO also acknowledges each successful Windows drop to TkDND so a later drag does not replace the accumulated value. A .txt file is preserved as the control-file path. Other dropped files contribute their containing folder. A comma inside a dropped path is preserved normally and does not trigger quoting; user-supplied quotes remain accepted.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
The upper-right hamburger menu includes Copy Requests, Redundancy Groups, and Donate. Donate > Venmo displays @James-Scarano-3. Donate > Check displays James Scarano, 49 Majestic Ave., Nashua, NH 03063. Venmo and Check are submenu items with chevrons. Their detail rows use normal dark menu text instead of disabled gray text. Copy Request Delete removes only the selected request's TLO state/report directory; The deletion guard validates the listed directory ID, requires state.json to agree with that containing directory, and refuses malformed, traversal, or out-of-root deletion targets. Copy Requests reject source trees containing symbolic links; TLO repeats that check immediately at the copy boundary and prevents a link that appears during the copy from being followed into external content.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
The GUI and CLI share the same defaults through the internal option registry. setlist.fm still depends on etreeDB: command-line startup rejects --setlistfm-lookup unless --etree-lookup is also supplied, and the GUI automatically selects etreeDB when setlist.fm is selected.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
When you click Inventory, the Inventory button grays out immediately, Tag and Add New Shows are disabled, and the output pane reports that the request was accepted while TLO prepares the roots. Existing labeled-volume overlap is resolved automatically from the same/parent/child/non-overlap path rules; TLO does not ask a Skip/Re-inventory question. Ctrl-A (Control-A) selects all text in the main output console. Ctrl-A also selects the complete contents of every editable text input field throughout the Main GUI, Search, Artist DB Search, and dialogs, including fields created after the main window opens.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
The main Inventory window also includes a Research button. Clicking Research opens a text-entry dialog for an artist followed by a date, a venue, or a date. Search opens the result in a separate scrollable text window. Research is read-only and searches TLOHome/logs/meta*.log plus related comp*.log entries using the same engine as the standalone tlo-research app.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Main hamburger > Search > Inventory, Artist, Cities, or Venues opens the appropriate search GUI and searches the clipboard text if present. searchAuxDBs has mutually exclusive Artist, City, and Venue checkboxes and queries artists.sqlite, cities.sqlite, or venues.sqlite in TLOHome/TLO_DBs. City accepts City or City, Region. Venue results preserve historical display names and show spelling strength and context. To reverse logged folder operations from Main, enter one fully qualified original, current, or containing folder path in Path(s), then choose hamburger > Reverse. The path may identify a missing original that is to be restored. Do not enter a list, wildcard, .txt control file, or inventory directive. TLO displays the exact path, TLOHome and intended operation in a confirmation box with No selected by default. Choose Yes to proceed; No or closing the dialog does nothing. With Dry Run checked, Yes previews the matching folder actions without changing folders or audio tags; a reverse audit is still recorded. The Main output displays the selected log and outcomes. Wait for Reverse to finish before starting another operation, installing an update, or quitting. Ordinary Reverse does not restore inventory records or original audio tags. The standalone tlo-reverse CLI accepts the same single folder path. TLO reads successful folder-operation mappings from TLOHome/logs, including the durable folderOperations.log archive that survives re-inventory and infers whether the operation was Rename Compliantly, Tag Copy, or Tag Copy/Delete Original; no copy-versus-rename flag is normally needed. Copy/Delete is restored to the exact logged original location/name, whether or not compliant renaming was enabled. Rename-only is renamed back. Tag Copy leaves the original in place and removes only the exact logged copy after confirming that the source and copy still contain the same relative file-name set. TLO also leaves a Tag Copy untouched when a copied file has a post-run modification timestamp that makes a later user replacement possible. Cross-filesystem reversal preserves symbolic links as symbolic links and verifies their link targets before the logged destination is removed. Audio tags are never changed or reversed. If a broad path matches more than one currently applicable run, Reverse stops instead of guessing; enter a narrower path or use the CLI --log option. Use Main Dry Run or CLI --dry-run to see the selected log and exact folder actions without changing folders. Results are appended to TLOHome/logs/reverseFolders.log. Any legacy GUI Skip/Re-inventory decision stays open until answered; closing that dialog selects Skip.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
If you click Quit while a full inventory scan is active, TLO warns that cleanup may take a moment, then requests cancellation and stops active workers. Cancellation cleanup removes only tokenized log files newly allocated by that interrupted run; a token/log set reused from an earlier inventory is preserved. Token values are validated before cleanup so a malformed value cannot escape TLOHome/logs. If scanning has already finished but cleanup, sibling-collection aggregation, or output generation is still running, TLO warns before a forced exit that an in-progress collection move can be interrupted. Collection moves are journaled, and the next normal Inventory run attempts automatic rollback before scanning.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl252\slmult1\sb0\sa60\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\qc\sb0\sa60\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\qc\sb0\sa60{\loch\fs18\i\loch
Figure 2. Review Operation reports the active settings before an action starts. For long settings lists, the Review Operation window now displays a vertical scrollbar; scroll to see all settings. Preview Changes, Go Back and Start stay visible at the bottom.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\rtlch\af12\afs26\ab \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\sl240\slmult1\keep\sb200\sa0\keepn\cf18\f4\fs26\b\dbch\af9\sl240\slmult1\sb180\sa80{\loch
Fields and destination selection}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Tag Copy and Tag Copy/Delete Original are mutually exclusive inventory copy modes. Checking either box only selects the mode. After you click Inventory, TLO opens the same destination-entry window and requires an existing full path with readable available-space information. Tag Copy states that originals are retained. Tag Copy/Delete Original adds the warning that originals are removed after verified transfer. Before any transfer, TLO builds the show group and extracts metadata from the original source tree, including original folder and path text, selected setlist content, music filenames, and existing audio tags. It then copies or moves the identified show folder and uses the destination path for tagging and final inventory output. The transfer always applies to the complete identified folder tree: every file and every folder beneath it is included, including non-music files and empty subfolders. Tag Copy always totals the source and checks destination capacity because it always creates another tree. Copy/Delete Original performs that source-size capacity check only when the destination is on a different partition; a same-partition Copy/Delete Original operation is a directory rename or move and does not total or compare file sizes. Tag Copy real copies are verified by relative file paths, file sizes, and descendant folder structure before tagging continues. During copying, the incomplete destination resides in a .tlo-partial-* sibling folder that TLO does not inventory. Only after this verification succeeds is the copy published under its normal final show name. A cancelled or terminated worker may leave a .tlo-partial-* work folder; it is not a complete copied show and should not be treated as one. Ordinary failures clean up the current operation's temporary folder. A destination that appears during publication is never overwritten, and TLO chooses a unique (copyN) or (altN) final name. Copy/Delete Original now also stages before publication: cross-filesystem copying checks the complete recursive tree and SHA-256 hashes in a .tlo-partial-* folder, publishes atomically, and only then removes the source. Same-filesystem Copy/Delete atomically moves the entire source folder to a .tlo-partial-* sibling and publishes it with a second atomic rename, without copying or hashing. If publication fails before completion, TLO tries to restore the original source name atomically. If restoration fails or a worker is killed between the two same-filesystem renames, the complete show may remain under a hidden .tlo-partial-* staging name. TLO creates a recovery journal before moving the original. To inspect without changing anything, run tlo-reverse.exe "DESTINATION_FOLDER" --recover-staging --dry-run. Only after examining the proposed source and destination, run tlo-reverse.exe "DESTINATION_FOLDER" --recover-staging to restore a stopped worker's recorded original path. It never overwrites an existing folder or guesses ownership of unjournaled partial directories. A journal belonging to a worker that might still be running, another host, or an ambiguous source/stage/destination state is reported as a conflict for manual inspection. Incomplete cross-volume staging is not recoverable as a complete same-volume show; do not delete or rename those partial copies without examination. If deletion of a verified cross-filesystem source fails, retain the verified published destination and review the remaining original. Cross-partition Tag Copy/Delete Original uses stricter deletion safety: the copied tree must match completely by relative structure and recursive SHA-256 content identity, and any read/stat/hash failure aborts source deletion. Same-partition Copy/Delete remains a direct directory move and does not perform unnecessary copy/hash verification. When a compliant Show/folder name collides with an existing same-base folder, TLO does not call the new folder a copy merely because the name is already present. It first compares the complete recursive trees. A (copyN) suffix is used when the recursive relative folder names, relative file names, and exact file sizes match. Contents are not hashed for duplicate classification. Different file names, exact file sizes or directory structures make the new item an (altN) instead; different bytes at the same file size do not. File timestamps do not affect this decision. An existing folder already named with (copyN) is also rechecked during inventory; if it does not exactly match another same-base sibling tree, the Show is identified with (altN) instead. For Tag Copy/Delete Original, the selected destination parent is only the container that receives transferred shows; it is not permission to re-inventory every previously inventoried show below that parent. Existing destination bootlist/setlist entries remain in place, and only an existing row whose complete destination volume and path exactly match a show path produced by the current Copy/Delete run may be replaced.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
If the destination cannot hold the required source data, the GUI shows an alert naming the destination, the source path or paths, and the additional space needed.}
