Pause}\cell\pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\intbl\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200{\loch
Pauses full inventory between filesystem operations. Does not apply to Add Shows.}\cell\row\pard \trowd\trql\trleft97\ltrrow\trpaddft3\trpaddt0\trpaddfl3\trpaddl0\trpaddfb3\trpaddb0\trpaddfr3\trpaddr0\clpadft3\clpadt108\clpadfr3\clpadr108\cellx5267\clpadft3\clpadt108\clpadfr3\clpadr108\cellx10436\pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\intbl\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200{\loch
Resume}\cell\pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\intbl\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa200{\loch
Resumes a paused full inventory. Repeated Resume clicks report only when a pause is active.}\cell\row\pard \pard\plain \s2\rtlch\af12\afs26\ab \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\sl240\slmult1\keep\sb200\sa0\keepn\cf18\f4\fs26\b\dbch\af9\sl240\slmult1\sb180\sa80{\loch
Performance modes}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Search-path worker count is capped by the number of search paths queued for that run. Postprocess setlist/bootlist export uses a separate bounded worker pool after scanning finishes. In the Main GUI, the automatic Max Workers ceiling follows the selected Performance Mode: gentle = 1, balanced = up to 2, fast = up to the CPU count, and extreme = up to four times the CPU count with a hard automatic ceiling of 64. At GUI startup, an explicitly supplied positive launcher value is shown initially; otherwise the selected mode's automatic ceiling is shown. Selecting a Performance Mode always resets Max Workers to that mode's automatic ceiling. You can then edit Max Workers to a lower or other positive ceiling; the actual worker count may still be lower because of drive grouping, available work, or correctness constraints. The worker scheduler also enforces one inventory-mutating run per TLOHome: a second Full Inventory against the same TLOHome is rejected before tokenized logs or inventory output are changed. Search and unrelated read-only operations can still run.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
gentle: best when using external drives, spinning disks, or Windows desktops where responsiveness matters. Uses 1 worker by default. On Windows the inventory process uses idle priority when supported. On Linux/WSL/macOS, only spawned worker processes are niced; the long-lived GUI/CLI parent stays at normal niceness.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
balanced: moderate parallelism. GUI and CLI default performance mode. Typically uses up to 2 workers by default. On Windows the inventory process uses below-normal priority when supported. On Linux/WSL/macOS, only spawned worker processes are niced.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
fast: higher normal throughput, usually up to the CPU count by default. Uses normal priority. On Windows, switching to Fast after Gentle or Balanced explicitly restores the process priority captured at application startup.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
extreme: speed-first for inventory traversal. It may request the most parallel work, but Max Workers remains the ceiling and physical-drive, work-group, and correctness constraints may still reduce the actual worker count. Postprocess remains bounded. Extreme uses normal priority; on Windows it restores the startup process priority after a lower-priority mode. Extreme does not skip non-compliant tag checks.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
TLO Main and Search load one shared TLOHome/tlo-preferences.ini at startup. If absent, TLO creates a default; an existing file is preserved. Save & Close validates and saves changes but does not change the running controls. Restart the program to apply the file. Boolean values accept true/false, yes/no, on/off, and 1/0.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\rtlch\af12\afs26\ab \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\sl240\slmult1\keep\sb200\sa0\keepn\cf18\f4\fs26\b\dbch\af9\sl240\slmult1\sb180\sa80{\rtlch\af14\afs24 \ltrch\loch\cf1\dbch\af14\loch
9.1 Editing startup preferences}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\rtlch\af14\afs24 \ltrch\loch\cf1\dbch\af14\loch
In TLO Main or Search, select Edit Preferences from the hamburger menu. The editor opens the single TLOHome/tlo-preferences.ini. Save & Close validates the entire file, saves it and closes the editor; Cancel abandons edits (after confirmation if necessary). Invalid values are reported and not saved. The running app keeps its currently selected settings, including fonts, until it is restarted. Restart Main or Search to apply new startup defaults. Existing files are not overwritten by installation or startup; CLI overrides still take precedence.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\rtlch\af14\afs24 \ltrch\loch\cf1\dbch\af14\loch
The [main] section controls checkboxes, including Proper Grammar (use Last, First or Band, The Artist DB forms for show names) and Deep Audio Check (decode every supported audio stream after a reversible tag probe). The latter is slow and can activate corruption-handling policy. Artist in Album Tag is the only checkbox checked by default in an uncustomized installation. The [general], [search], [manual_updates], and [add_new_shows] sections configure the remaining windows and shared settings.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60\rtlch\af14\afs24 \ltrch\loch\cf1\dbch\af14\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60\rtlch\af14\afs24 \ltrch\loch\cf1\dbch\af14\loch

\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af12\afs32\ab \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\sl240\slmult1\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn\cf17\f4\fs32\b\dbch\af9\sl240\slmult1\sb240\sa120{\loch
10. Compliant versus non-compliant mode}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl252\slmult1\sb0\sa60\loch

\par \pard\plain \s2\rtlch\af12\afs26\ab \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\sl240\slmult1\keep\sb200\sa0\keepn\cf18\f4\fs26\b\dbch\af9\sl240\slmult1\sb180\sa80{\loch
Compliant mode}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl252\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Use Compliant mode when folders intentionally follow predictable naming. The main supported forms, in precedence order, are String1 Date String2, String1 - String2 Date when a complete date occurs at a String2 boundary, String1 - String2, and String1 Date. For Date, TLO first looks for yyyy-mm-dd-style dates, including legitimate partial forms such as 19xx-xx-xx and 20xx-xx-xx, same-month multi-day forms such as 1970-04-12-13 or 1970-04-12,13, and then accepted year ranges before falling back to older date-recognition rules. A same-month multi-day range may contain the first day plus one or two trailing day values; the days must be valid calendar dates and strictly ascend. Textual equivalents such as 12-13 April 1970, April 12-13, 1970, and 1970 April 12-13 are also accepted. TLO uses the first day as the canonical Date and preserves the complete original multi-day range in PARENTHETICALS. Existing year-range behavior is unchanged. Century-known 19xx/20xx partial years require the remaining month/day components to stay unknown. In compliant mode, FLAC and MP3 metadata tags are not read or used. Compliant cannot be combined with Rename Compliantly.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
String1 Date String2: String1 is the artist text, Date is normalized, and String2 is kept as the trailing show text unless online lookup fills venue/location. By default, a matched alias is written using the Artist DB master name. Check As-Is Artist Name to preserve String1 as written.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
String1 - String2 Date: after String1 Date String2 fails, TLO checks whether String2 begins or ends with one complete performance date. The date is normalized and assigned as Date, while the remaining String2 text stays raw as the album/show/venue tail. Example: The Georgia Satellites - Santa Cruz 01-17-87 becomes The Georgia Satellites 1987-01-17 Santa Cruz. Bare years, ranges, partial dates, and dates embedded in the middle of String2 do not trigger this refinement.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
String1 - String2: when the boundary-date refinement does not match, String2 is treated as an album/show tail and no Date is assigned. A parenthetical-only tail such as (Live) is preserved.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Artist/Album dash spacing: once the complete leading artist text resolves uniquely in the Artist DB, the first Artist/Album dash may have spaces on both sides, one side, or neither side. Artist - Album, Artist- Album, Artist -Album, and Artist-Album are therefore equivalent only for that DB-confirmed artist boundary. Later dashes remain part of String2; arbitrary hyphens are not globally treated as separators.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Every final Show Name retains all trailing parenthetical items in their original order. TLO captures them from the original folder name before cleaning the path for artist, date, venue, and location parsing, then restores them after normalization. A recognized performance qualifier such as Early Show or Late Show is also retained as a parenthesized suffix when it is not already present. This applies to Full Inventory, Add Shows, Dry Run output, folder renames, bootlist rows, setlist names, and Album tags. For example, Danny Gatton 1994-03-13 Bottom Line NYC (set 2) becomes Danny Gatton 1994-03-13 Bottom Line New York, NY (set 2).}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
String1 Date: fallback when no String2 exists; show name becomes Artist Date. Artist output follows the As-Is Artist Name checkbox.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Standard video wrapper folders such as VIDEO_TS or BDMV can resolve from parent path components.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
The Billboard MP3 year-folder special case applies only when all inventoried music files are .mp3, the folder name is a four-digit year from 1959 through 2005, and billboard appears somewhere in the path. The exported setlist filename for this special case uses the same four-digit year.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
City identification uses physical TLO_DBs/cities.sqlite and documented aliases. Invalid or ambiguous additions must not override reliable artist evidence, and recognized city names do not imply an unspecified state or country.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\rtlch\af12\afs26\ab \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\sl240\slmult1\keep\sb200\sa0\keepn\cf18\f4\fs26\b\dbch\af9\sl240\slmult1\sb180\sa80{\loch
Non-compliant mode}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl252\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Use Non-compliant mode when folder names vary or contain partial or uncertain metadata. Non-compliant mode uses more evidence and tries to fill missing metadata without overwriting earlier trusted fields. As-Is Artist Name applies here too: unchecked normally uses an Artist DB master name for an unambiguous alias match; checked preserves the artist text from the metadata source that identified the show. Proper Grammar is directly beneath As-Is Artist Name and defaults unchecked. When Proper Grammar is checked and As-Is Artist Name is unchecked, TLO prefers a qualifying Artist DB alias such as Smith, John or Kinks, The after the artist has been identified. If no qualifying proper-grammar alias exists, the normal current naming result is used. As-Is Artist Name always overrides Proper Grammar. The effective Proper Grammar spelling is used for Show names, tags, Artist-in-Album prefixes, generated/exported metadata, and Rename Compliantly.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Samples ALBUMARTIST, ARTIST, ALBUM, and DATE metadata from up to two supported FLAC or MP3 files in non-compliant mode. Within the audio-tag tier, ALBUMARTIST is considered before ARTIST. Before Artist DB lookup, an explicitly dash-delimited or parenthesized terminal performance qualifier such as Early Show or Late Show is separated from a tag artist candidate. If the normal exact lookup then misses, TLO may ignore apostrophe characters for one retry, but accepts the result only when it identifies one unique Artist DB master. Tag values containing unknown or track are treated as blank for artist purposes. Artist evidence precedence is: inline --/slam; strong explicit path/folder/commercial evidence; DB-confirmed structured selected-setlist Artist metadata; DB-backed selected-setlist filename evidence; DB-matched FLAC/MP3 tag artist; and finally an unmatched raw tag artist. Structured setlist Artist metadata may replace only weak generic path/subdirectory evidence under its established confidence safeguards.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Searches folder and path text for artist, date, venue, and location clues, including known artist names and aliases. Generic path scanning does not promote an Artist DB term found entirely inside the parsed venue/location tail of a date-bearing folder; Attic inside Eddie's Attic is therefore venue text, while an exact artist directory named Attic remains eligible. Short no-space-date artist abbreviations such as gd in gd1977-05-08 are deliberately weak evidence: TLO first checks stronger tags, full DB-backed path names, selected-setlist metadata/filename evidence, and other structured sources. Only if those fail may the abbreviation resolve through artists.sqlite as a final local fallback; an enabled artist/date online lookup may corroborate it but does not promote abbreviation evidence above stronger sources.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Recognizes the non-compliant Date Artist Venue Location path pattern when a date begins the folder component. The Artist must be the longest unique Artist DB match beginning immediately after the date. A venue must remain after removing the Artist, and the remainder must also parse into City plus Region or Country. For example, 1997-04-05 Genesis Old Pub London England becomes Genesis 1997-04-05 Old Pub London, England. TLO rejects 1990-07-10 Palace Melbourne Australia under this pattern because removing Palace leaves only a location and no venue.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Also recognizes a narrowly guarded non-compliant Artist + Place + Date + recognized-tail form after the ordinary path rules have had their chance. For example, if RTF is already an alias in artists.sqlite, RTF Paris 7 March 76 flac16 can resolve to Return to Forever 1976-03-07 Paris. Todd Snider Skipper's Smokehouse, Tampa, FL 2005-04-16 sdb can resolve to Todd Snider 2005-04-16 Skipper's Smokehouse Tampa, FL when Todd Snider is a direct unique Artist DB prefix match. TLO does not invent RTF from Return to Forever; it also does not invent other artist initials or use fuzzy/embedded artist text for this fallback. Text after the date must be fully recognized as media/source technical text such as flac16, flac24, shn, wav, 16-bit, 24-bit, SBD, SDB, AUD, FM, matrix, soundboard, or audience, or be one existing terminal performance qualifier such as Set 1, Early Show, Late Show, Soundcheck, or Rehearsal. SDB is accepted here only as a narrow source-token compatibility spelling and does not create qualifier metadata. Existing qualifier handling remains authoritative. State/country-anchored place text and exact venues.txt matches may be used immediately. A single unanchored word such as Paris is only a last-resort display/location hint after eTreeDB, selected setlist metadata, and setlist.fm fail to provide stronger venue/location information. Unknown multi-word text and ordinary title-like post-date text do not activate this fallback. Existing Artist Date ... parsing retains precedence, and Compliant mode is unchanged.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
For an ordinary non-compliant String1 - String2 name, TLO checks String2 for a complete performance date before using the generic Artist - Album fallback. It also accepts a supported partial date when that date is at the beginning or end of String2; a partial date embedded in the middle of album-like text does not force performance mode. After removing the accepted date and its surrounding dash separators, conservative venue/location parsing may establish performance metadata only when city plus state/region or country is strongly anchored and no unexplained trailing text remains. For example, Ray Price - Gilley's - Pasadena, Texas - xx-xx-81 becomes Ray Price 1981-xx-xx Gilley's Pasadena, TX. Explicit leading-year Artist - Album commercial-release forms keep their separate rules, and ambiguous album-like text remains an album rather than being forced into performance metadata.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Missing spaces around the first Artist/Album dash do not prevent album/collection identification when the complete text before that dash is a unique Artist DB match. TLO splits only that artist-delimiting dash. For example, Van Morrison-Saint Dominic's Preview-Hard Nose The Highway Live becomes Artist Van Morrison with album Saint Dominic's Preview-Hard Nose The Highway Live; the later dash stays in the album title. If the left side is unknown or ambiguous, TLO does not enable this relaxed unspaced-dash rule.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Commercial releases may use (YYYY) - Artist - Album or YYYY - Artist - Album, where YYYY is exactly one four-digit 19xx or 20xx year. TLO uses that year only to recognize the commercial-release form; it does not write the year to DATE or include it in the Show Name. Complete dates, partial dates, year ranges, two-digit years, x-filled years, and years embedded in other text do not qualify. The Show Name is Artist - Album. If the album tail already begins with the same resolved Artist followed by a dash, TLO removes that one redundant Artist component, so Rod Stewart - Rod Stewart - Camouflage becomes Rod Stewart - Camouflage. For tagging, the discovered Album portion is retained as ALBUM_NAME and is used as the base Album tag even when Date, Venue, and Location are blank; it must not fall through to Unknown.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Aggregates related wrapper/part folders such as CD1/CD2/CD3 under one logical show only when every direct music-bearing child under the parent belongs to the same release relationship. If any direct music-bearing sibling is unrelated, TLO keeps the folders separate and never promotes the broad collection/search parent into one show. A short v-number inside a larger descriptive parenthetical, such as (WLIR-FM, rm v.2), is not treated as a volume suffix. The narrow Parent/Parent (1) through Parent (N) multipart-release layout is aggregated only when all direct music-bearing children share the parent-matching base, the part numbers are consecutive from 1, and the parent itself has no direct music. Inventory discovery can continue below a recorded parent music folder so a genuinely independent nested opening act can still become its own show; normal wrapper/grouping rules prevent ordinary discs/sets from being split incorrectly.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Recognizes one additional guarded wrapper layer, such as Show/FLAC/Set 1 and Show/FLAC/Set 2. Set, Disc, Disk, CD, D, Part, Side, and Tape numbered folders may be combined under a non-numbered media container such as FLAC or Audio, and a setlist stored directly in the date-bearing Show folder applies to the complete show. TLO crosses only that validated wrapper layer and checks only files directly in the Show folder. It does not rescan sibling show folders. If unrelated music also exists below the proposed Show folder, TLO does not promote that folder into one show.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
For a date-bearing show, recognizable supplemental/source/media descendants such as Source 2 EXTRAS, Right Channel (Soundboard Feed), Open Mic Lithium, art-docs, Concert Pictures/Video, Disc 1/Flac, and Disc end tracks remain part of the dated show instead of becoming accidental independent shows. TLO keeps the descendant separate when it carries its own complete date or conflicting tagged Artist/AlbumArtist evidence, and it does not absorb a supplemental branch when another unrelated music-bearing branch such as an opening act exists below the dated show. If direct root audio and wrapper/supplemental descendants initially resolve to the same physical show root, they are collapsed into one logical group before tagging or renaming so two workers cannot independently mutate the same folder.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
During a real non-compliant Full Inventory, TLO can consolidate validated non-date sibling collections such as Frankie Valli - Greatest D1/D2 or Pink Floyd - Early Flights/(alt1)/(alt2) into one parent named Frankie Valli - Greatest or Pink Floyd - Early Flights. Dry Run never creates or moves this parent and blocks if an interrupted earlier consolidation requires recovery. A complete performance date excludes the rule. For this sibling-collection decision, xxxx-xx-xx is also treated as a specific date and prevents consolidation, avoiding accidental grouping of distinct unknown-date shows. A year, year span, decade label, or no year is still allowed.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
TLO compares only sibling candidates in the current search on the same partition. D/CD-style suffixes are multipart evidence. alt folders are included only when usable setlist or filename titles show materially different content; identical, overlapping, shifted, or insufficient song lists remain separate alternatives. No other partition is searched for this decision.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
TLO creates a journaled temporary sibling parent at the same level as the collection members, moves only the accepted members into it, concatenates usable member setlists into parent info.txt, and renames the completed aggregate to the shared release name. For an alt family that contains the unsuffixed base folder, TLO stages that original member as (alt0), so TLO creates Pink Floyd - Early Flights/Pink Floyd - Early Flights (alt0), (alt1), (alt2), and so on instead of ever creating Pink Floyd - Early Flights/Pink Floyd - Early Flights. alt0 remains first in collection-wide track order. A collision or failed operation rolls back to the exact original sibling names. If rollback itself fails, TLO reports that recovery is incomplete, logs the suppressed rollback error, and names the current recovery container instead of hiding the failure. The reversal utility recovers the interrupted case where an older completed outer collection folder has the same pathname as one of the original member folders, and The recovery procedure makes that recovery portable between native Windows and WSL path forms. Existing recovery journals remain supported.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
If automatic collection recovery cannot proceed safely, TLO now prints the exact recovery container, journal file, planned final folder when available, and each member's original and staged paths with its current state. ORIGINAL_ONLY and STAGED_ONLY show which side currently exists; BOTH or MISSING means TLO will not guess. Close programs that may be holding the folders open and run Inventory again. If the same error repeats, do not delete the journal or manually merge the recovery folder; preserve the listed paths for review. Dry Run remains read-only and tells you to run a normal inventory to perform automatic recovery.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
The new parent and info.txt are inventoried once; the original children remain intact beneath it. Files are ordered one child at a time, including layouts such as D1/FLAC and D2/FLAC, and tagged with one continuous 01-N sequence, or 001-N when there are more than 99 tracks.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Uses selected setlist header metadata for date, artist, venue, and location fallback before track names or hash/checksum sections. Supported strong header shapes include strict unlabeled Artist / Venue, City, State-or-Country / Date; compact Artist Date and Artist Date Venue lines where the date anchors the split; concise Location -- Venue or Venue -- Location; and Artist followed by Live at Venue, City, State/Region/Country, Date. Introductory prose cannot shift the Artist/Venue slots, and a trailing performance date on a Live at line is removed before Venue/Location splitting. Before Artist DB lookup, an explicitly separated terminal performance qualifier is removed from the structured artist candidate, and the same unique-only apostrophe retry used for tag artists is available after an exact miss. An unlabeled or compact artist is accepted only when it resolves uniquely in artists.sqlite. A DB-confirmed structured setlist artist may correct a conflicting artist supported only by weaker generic path/subdirectory scanning, but it does not override /slam or strong explicit commercial/folder artist evidence. Structured selected-setlist Artist metadata is evaluated before selected-setlist-filename and FLAC/MP3 tag artist evidence. A single-word Artist DB hit embedded in a selected-setlist filename is accepted only when the filename has strong positional structure, such as the date being the first substantive component after the artist. Location parsing requires the state/region/country to terminate the location expression, rejects title-like city fragments ending in connectives such as In, At, On, Of, To, or From, accepts safe mixed-case state codes only in strongly delimited forms, and avoids pulling very short venue words out of unrelated longer lines or comma-separated performer lists. Canadian postal abbreviations AB, BC, MB, NB, NL, NS, NT, NU, ON, PE, QC, SK, and YT are also recognized as region anchors. Uppercase remains the default for newly inferred protected two-letter codes. In non-compliant path parsing only, an exact lowercase code may be accepted when the path-derived location fragment is consistently lowercase; mixed-case forms remain rejected. Common/word-like codes such as in, on, or, me, and hi additionally require either a comma before the code or both a venue and city, with noise-word guards retained. Selected-setlist/free-form parsing remains uppercase-only. Unambiguous Canadian regions retain Canada as Country, and NT does not infer a country by itself because the abbreviation is shared with Australia. A date followed by a double-dash duration remains valid date evidence. Ordinary text sampling is bounded to 1 MiB, full ordinary-text reads above 16 MiB are rejected, and DOCX document.xml content above 16 MiB uncompressed is rejected before decompression.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Artist DB terminal-suffix fallback is guarded. TLO queries the complete candidate first. Only after a complete-name miss may it strip one terminal Band, Group, All Star/All-Star/All Stars/All-Stars, or the corresponding ... Band form and retry. The shorter name must resolve uniquely and nonterminal occurrences are never stripped. When that fallback succeeds, TLO restores the exact removed suffix for the performance artist unless the DB master already ends in a supported grouping suffix. Restored performance names that are not themselves full Artist DB masters are added to TLOHome/artistsNotInDatabase.txt for review.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Uses eTreeDB and setlist.fm only when enabled, and only to fill blanks or report observations.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Commas in unnumbered song lists do not need to be followed by spaces; TLO treats \u8220\'93Song A,Song B,Song C\u8221\'94 the same as \u8220\'93Song A, Song B, Song C\u8221\'94 when the item count matches the audio files.}
\par \trowd\trqc\ltrrow\trkeep\trpaddft3\trpaddt0\trpaddfl3\trpaddl0\trpaddfb3\trpaddb0\trpaddfr3\trpaddr0\clpadft3\clpadt108\clpadfr3\clpadr108\clcbpat23\cellx8316\pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\intbl\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa20{\loch
Practical choice: When in doubt, start with non-compliant mode for mixed real-world collections. Use compliant mode when you deliberately organized the folders to match the supported naming patterns and want those names trusted.}\cell\row\pard \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl252\slmult1\sb0\sa60\loch

\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af12\afs32\ab \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\sl240\slmult1\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn\cf17\f4\fs32\b\dbch\af9\sl240\slmult1\sb240\sa120{\loch
11. Online lookup options}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl252\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Online lookup is optional. It normally fills missing venue/location fields after artist and a full yyyy-mm-dd date are known. For conflicting complete dates involving audio DATE tags with online lookup enabled, TLO checks every distinct candidate (up to four) against each enabled provider, compares selected setlist song order and independent venue/city clues, and logs its reasoning. Tags are not trusted solely because they repeat. An unresolved conflict blocks ordinary automatic tagging, renaming, and transfer. A service error does not establish that a concert did not happen. Normally, later sources do not overwrite nonblank metadata found earlier. In non-compliant mode, one narrow correction is allowed: higher-confidence selected-setlist venue/location metadata may replace a conflicting value supported only by weaker path-part evidence. eTreeDB and other stronger evidence are not displaced. Other non-equivalent disagreements are reported as observations in the summary log.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
etreeDB checkbox or --etree-lookup: uses eTreeDB first among online sources. When tagging is enabled, the same option can also allow a last-resort eTreeDB song-title lookup if local setlist titles are not usable.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
setlist.fm checkbox or --setlistfm-lookup: in normal matching, uses setlist.fm as a last resort when etreeDB does not return usable venue/location data. On the command line, --setlistfm-lookup requires --etree-lookup. In the GUI, selecting setlist.fm automatically selects etreeDB. When Thorough Setlist Matching is enabled, setlist.fm becomes a proactive evidence source when enabled: TLO may query it even after etreeDB succeeds and may collect additional same-date candidates so they can corroborate or challenge the local/eTree setlist. This broader coverage does not give setlist.fm higher authority.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
setlist.fm requires SETLISTFM_API_KEY in the environment. setlist.fm Upgrade additionally requires SETLISTFMUPGRADE_API_KEY or SETLISTFM_UPGRADE_API_KEY, set to the same key value. Explicit process/session values take precedence; persisted Windows User/System values are fallback only for missing/blank process values.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Normal setlist.fm access is rate-limited to one request every 600 milliseconds across local workers and capped at 1,400 calls per inventory run. If both setlist.fm and setlist.fm Upgrade are checked, TLO instead permits up to 14 requests per second and enforces a shared maximum of 48,000 requests per UTC day across setlist.fm clients and workers using the same TLOHome coordination state. Independent simultaneous inventory-mutating runs against that TLOHome are blocked separately by the inventory-instance lock. Thorough Setlist Matching does not raise these limits by itself: with setlist.fm enabled but upgrade off, better coverage can take substantially longer and may be curtailed by the normal rate/run limits. With upgrade on, Thorough uses the same deconfliction rules but can gather setlist.fm evidence much more aggressively. Upgrade changes coverage/capacity, never setlist.fm authority. Coordination state is stored under TLOHome/logs/setlistfm-state rather than the system temporary directory. The rate limiter treats a persisted last-request timestamp more than one configured interval in the future as corrupt and clamps any spacing sleep to no more than one configured interval. If a worker cannot acquire the reservation lock within 20 seconds, TLO records the lookup failure and continues without waiting indefinitely.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
For U.S. lookup results, show names use Artist Date Venue City StateCode. Non-U.S. results use Artist Date Venue City Country.}
\par \pard\plain \s2\rtlch\af12\afs26\ab \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\sl240\slmult1\keep\sb200\sa0\keepn\cf18\f4\fs26\b\dbch\af9\sl240\slmult1\sb180\sa80{\loch
Thorough Setlist Matching}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Thorough Setlist Matching is a separate accuracy-versus-time choice. It defaults off. With it off, TLO preserves early stopping and the familiar local setlist, eTreeDB, then setlist.fm fallback behavior. With it on, TLO continues gathering reasonable local candidates plus whichever online lookup sources are enabled; etreeDB is included only when etreeDB is checked, and setlist.fm only when setlist.fm is checked. In the main-window Info area, Thorough itself produces no extra message when neither online source is selected; when online sources are selected, the message names only those enabled sources. TLO compares exact-count and near-count (plus or minus two) lists, uses filename and existing Title-tag corroboration, Venue/Location compatibility, and independent-source agreement, and can align recording-only Intro/Tuning/Crowd items or performance-only Encore items without shifting every later track. A strong recording-local list can still beat online consensus when the actual audio supports it; matching eTreeDB and setlist.fm data can beat a weak local parse. If the best two cross-source candidates are nearly tied but materially different, TLO reports an ambiguity rather than silently choosing by precedence.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
When setlist.fm is enabled, Thorough makes setlist.fm proactive even after a usable eTreeDB answer and can inspect additional same-date results. This can take substantially longer under normal setlist.fm access because the ordinary 600-millisecond spacing and 1,400-call run cap still apply. setlist.fm Upgrade removes that major coverage/time constraint by allowing the existing upgraded 14-requests-per-second / 48,000-per-UTC-day capacity. Upgrade affects accessibility and coverage only; it never gives setlist.fm greater authority in the final decision.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Command-line equivalent: --thorough-setlist-matching.}
\par \pard\plain \s72\rtlch\af12\afs17\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af8\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\li360\lin360\ri144\rin144\sb40\sa80\ltrpar\cf0\f8\fs17\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af13\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\fi0\li360\lin360\ri144\rin144\sb60\sa120{\loch
Command-line rule: --compliant and --rename-compliantly cannot be used together; TLO reports an argument error.\line Command-line examples:\line tlo-gi.exe --TLOHome C:\\TLOHome --etree-lookup\line python3 tlo-gi.py --TLOHome "$TLOHome" --compliant --as-is-artist-name --etree-lookup --setlistfm-lookup}
\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af12\afs32\ab \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\sl240\slmult1\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn\cf17\f4\fs32\b\dbch\af9\sl240\slmult1\sb240\sa120{\loch
12. Tagging functions}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Use the Tag button on the main Inventory window when you want TLO to write tags to audio files without running a full inventory. The Tag action does not open a separate Tag window. GUI Tag uses the master Path(s), including inline --/slam directives, the selected Tag In Place / Tag Copy / Tag Copy/Delete Original mode, and all current main-window lookup, corruption, tagging, Dry Run, Performance Mode, and Max Workers settings. Progress and output stay in the main window. The standalone tlo-tag command-line application remains available for direct command-line tagging.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\qc\sb0\sa60\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\qc\sb0\sa60\loch

\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
GUI Tag runs directly from the main Inventory window. It uses every parsed master Path(s) job, including any inline --/slam directive, and current main-window settings. Select Tag In Place, Tag Copy, or Tag Copy/Delete Original before clicking Tag. Tag Copy and Tag Copy/Delete Original use the same destination prompt, capacity checks, verification, and source-retention/removal safeguards as Inventory. FLAC and MP3 are both first-class taggable formats and use the same metadata/title/track assignment flow. Tag writing failures are classified as corrupt unless caused by transient access; optional Deep Audio Check performs full-stream validation after a successful tag write. When Dry Run is selected, Tag resolves metadata, track titles, copy/move/rename plans, and SHN conversion targets without changing files. The main Current Operation panel, Pause/Resume controls, output console, and completion summary are reused for Tag. Standalone tlo-tag remains available and requires an explicit command-line search folder.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Before an actual tag save, TLO compares the exact normalized Artist, Album, Title, and Track Number it intends to write with the current file. With Delete Extra Tags unchecked, when those values already match and no stale TRACKTOTAL, DISCNUMBER, or DISCTOTAL metadata needs removal, the file is reported unchanged and is not rewritten; unrelated existing metadata is preserved. With Delete Extra Tags checked, TLO clears tag metadata and writes back only Artist, Album, Track Number, and Title, removing Album Artist, date/year, genre, comments, encoder fields, disc/total fields, and embedded tag artwork where supported. In that mode, a file is unchanged only when the four retained fields already match and there is no other tag metadata or artwork to remove. Rename Compliantly uses the same no-op principle, but exact spelling and capitalization are significant: Full Inventory, Add Shows, GUI Tag, and standalone tlo-tag skip the filesystem rename only when the final compliant folder name exactly matches the current name. A capitalization-only difference, such as dave van ronk becoming Dave Van Ronk after Artist DB normalization, is a real rename. On case-insensitive filesystems TLO applies such a change through a unique temporary sibling and then the exact compliant name, so the case change is recorded without creating an alternate/copy suffix. If the final rename and its rollback both fail, TLO reports the temporary sibling path that currently holds the folder and records the rollback error in diagnostics.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Standalone tlo-tag requires an explicit search folder and does not copy or move show folders; its only source-file replacement is verified Convert shn. Its corrupt-folder policy defaults to never (keep corrupt folders) unless --corrupt-folders all or threshold is explicitly supplied. GUI Tag follows the selected main tag mode: Tag In Place mutates the source, Tag Copy creates and verifies a separate copy, and Tag Copy/Delete Original safely moves or verified-copies the show before removing the source. Failed SHN conversions leave the source file in place.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
On native Windows installations, GUI Tag uses the same main Path(s) drag-and-drop behavior as Inventory. Multiple dropped folders or .txt control files are appended under the normal Path(s) rules. There is no separate GUI Tagging Path field.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Track-title safety: when a numbered setlist begins with a bare track number such as 01 and the next meaningful numbered song row confirms 02, TLO keeps track 01 with Title Unknown and continues the numbered list. A short numeric-only row cannot start an unstructured unnumbered-title block; numeric song names such as 69 and 1999 remain valid when embedded inside an established song block. Compact personnel credits such as Gary Burton(vib) or Chick Corea (p), and duration summaries such as min 38:24 are not unnumbered song titles. A convincing run of at least three consecutive numbered song rows still blocks arbitrary later prose from being scavenged merely because its line count matches the audio files. Before a shorter numbered candidate is padded with Unknown titles, however, TLO now checks for a plausible unnumbered song-list candidate whose count exactly matches the audio files. If numbered and unnumbered candidates both match the audio count, matching audio filenames and existing Title tags only reinforce a candidate; missing or unrelated filename/tag values are neutral. A short numbered block embedded in surrounding collector prose may lose an otherwise unsupported equal-count tie to a contiguous unnumbered song block. For an explicit Setlist:, Songs:, or Tracks: section containing one song per line, Notes: and other track-list terminators end the song section before later numbered collector notes can be interpreted as tracks. Legitimate titles beginning with Thank remain valid; prose such as Thanks to the taper remains a terminator. E:, Enc:, and Encore: prefixes are removed from an encore song title. Multi-disc filenames that include both disc and track tokens, including punctuation-separated forms such as CJM-Disc01,Track01.flac, are ordered by disc first and track second so disc 1 completes before disc 2 and written Track Number tags do not alternate odd/even between discs. The same ordering applies to validated multipart release folders named Parent (1) through Parent (N): the parenthesized part number is ordered first and each folder's filename track number second, so part 1 completes before part 2 and written Track Number tags do not stripe all track 01 files across the parts. Explicit Set 1, Disc 1, Disk 1, CD 1, Setlist 1, Set List 1, Track List 1, Songs:, and Tracks: headings mark the beginning of an unnumbered song region. When one is present, artist/venue/lineage text above the first boundary is not eligible as song titles just because adding those lines would make the count match the audio files. Blank lines, later Set/Disc/Disk/CD boundaries, and a bare Encore heading can continue the same ordered region. A date-labelled filler divider such as on July 7, 1967: is skipped as a title while following songs remain eligible. Notes/source/checksum/technical markers and decorative separators end the region. Valid date-prefixed broadcast/show dividers with trailing context, for example 30.10.1948 - Royal Roost, NY - WMCA Radio broadcast, are section boundaries and are not track 30. In an established numbered run, if TLO provisionally sees a higher number but the next numbered row resumes the exact number that was expected, the higher row is discarded as a false positive; 01-07, a date-like 30 row, then 08 therefore continues with track 08. Confirmed large gaps remain valid when later numbering reinforces them, such as 01, 02, 30, 31. Artist/header safety: a DB artist word embedded only as the trailing city of a longer path location such as Washington, DC is not used as generic path artist evidence, although an exact artist directory named Washington remains valid. Structured unlabeled headers may contain Artist / room-or-hall / named Venue / Location / Date; TLO keeps the first line as artist and the deeper named venue as Venue. A collaboration such as Chick Corea and Herbie Hancock may be accepted without a combined Artist DB row only when every component resolves uniquely in the Artist DB. Bare-number title safety: when one contiguous bare-number block exactly matches the audio-file count, the numbers are treated as track positions and the Titles are Unknown; the numeric values themselves are never written as song titles.\line The tagger also creates CorruptFlacs.txt in TLOHome. If a FLAC or MP3 file cannot be opened or written during tagging, TLO appends the complete file path to that file so bad audio files can be reviewed separately from other tagging errors. The historical filename is retained for compatibility. Exact placeholder titles Title, Titled, or Titles become Unknown after normal cleanup, including numbered forms such as 01. Title; longer legitimate titles containing those words are left unchanged. Machine-generated extraction/analysis tables are never accepted as song lists merely because their rows form a clean numeric sequence. Exact Audio Copy (EAC) TOC/extraction rows and other timestamp/sector-style multi-column records are technical data, and EAC markers start a technical tail that is not mined for titles. An explicit Songs: or Tracks: block is still retained as real local list evidence even when its count differs from the audio-file count; TLO reports the mismatch instead of truncating, shifting, or inventing a one-to-one mapping. When an apostrophe-style duration is accidentally joined to a valid dated subsection heading because of a lost newline, TLO can retain the song row while removing the attached dated-divider text.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60\loch

\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
TLO supplies its own FFmpeg converter. For SHN conversion, TLO validates decoding and FLAC audio equivalence before placing a verified original in Trash (or a recoverable same-volume fallback if Trash is unavailable). Failed verification retains the source. Distributed FFmpeg includes corresponding source and applicable LGPL notices.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af12\afs32\ab \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\sl240\slmult1\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn\cf17\f4\fs32\b\dbch\af9\sl240\slmult1\sb240\sa120{\loch
13. Add Shows incremental updater}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Add Shows Process New Shows reads new folders from the Search Path shown in the Add New Shows window. Search Path is pre-filled with TLOHome/readyForXfer, can be replaced with another existing folder, and supports native-Windows folder drag/drop. The immediate child folders of Search Path are treated as the new shows. Potential duplicate or upgrade folders remain under TLOHome/dups. Accepted folders are moved into TLOHome/staged after processing.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\qc\sb0\sa60{\loch\fs18\i\loch
Figure 3. Add Shows window. Dry Run and shared settings come from the main Inventory window.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Enter a nonblank Current Backup/Storage Drive and Volume. The TLOCurrentStorage environment variable or --current-storage-volume can prepopulate it.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Add Shows has no separate Preview buttons, no Dry Run checkbox, and no duplicated main-option checkboxes. Check for Duplicates remains because it applies only to Process New Shows. Select Dry Run on the main Inventory window, then click Process New Shows or Process Potential Duplicate/Upgrades to run the detailed non-destructive analysis. Each action reads the current main-window values when clicked and shows the same complete checkbox-value section in Review Operation before starting.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
After you click Start, TLO appends the reviewed settings to TLOHome/logs/runSettings.log before the action begins. Clicking Go Back or closing Review Operation does not create a log entry. If TLO cannot write the settings log, it reports the problem and does not start the action.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Add Shows does not keep a separate Mode display of copied main-window settings. Review Operation reports every current main-window checkbox in the same labels and order used for Full Inventory and Tag: etreeDB, Compliant, Tag In Place, Artist In Album Tag, setlist.fm, setlist.fm Upgrade, Thorough Setlist Matching, Rename Compliantly, Tag Copy, Convert shn, As-Is Artist Name, Tag Copy/Delete Original, Delete Extra Tags, and Dry Run. If Thorough and setlist.fm are on without upgrade, Review Operation warns that the normal 600-ms / 1,400-call limits can constrain coverage; with upgrade on it notes that upgraded access is available for proactive corroboration/deconfliction. Tag Copy and Tag Copy/Delete Original are reported but marked as not used by Add Shows.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Tag In Place is honored for accepted new and duplicate-resolution folders. Tag Copy and Tag Copy/Delete Original are ignored because Add Shows already has its own selected Search Path, dups, and staged movement workflow.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
If bootlist.csv does not exist, TLO warns that Add Shows is normally used after Full Inventory and asks whether to create the first bootlist from the selected Search Path.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
For potential duplicates/upgrades, review the matching existing rows and text files, then choose which old folders to keep or remove. The generated deleteReplacedFolders.bat commands use the selected old bootlist paths. Windows cleanup output keeps each displayed volume label inside a double-quoted field so punctuation in a label is treated as literal text rather than cmd.exe syntax. When running deleteReplacedFolders.bat, every labeled Windows cleanup entry verifies the actual drive label before any permanent rmdir /s /q command; disconnected drives, mismatches, missing label probes, and unlabeled entries are SKIPPED, with unlabeled delete lines commented out. To edit a drive letter, change the TLO_DELETE_TARGET assignment for that entry (not the display comment); the volume probe and rmdir share this path variable. Its expected label is encoded; generate a new entry when targeting a different labeled backup volume. Run the batch only after reviewing the targets. It permanently deletes old folders; it has no undo or Recycle Bin action. Immediate Delete New / Keep Old in the GUI is separate and requests the Recycle Bin. On POSIX/WSL, deleteBackupFolders.sh requires a verifiable volume identity before an active delete command is emitted. If the recorded volume is unlabeled, TLO writes a non-destructive SKIPPED/manual-verification entry and leaves the example rm -rf command commented out; verify the volume manually before enabling that line.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Add Shows potential duplicates: When Check for Duplicates is enabled, every potential duplicate goes to TLOHome/dups, even when an existing recording is on another or disconnected volume. Process New Shows does not append (pdupNN), rename, tag, convert, add inventory rows, or generate setlists for potential duplicates. Use Process Potential Duplicate/Upgrades to review them later. Older inventory rows with (pdupNN) remain recognizable for matching.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af12\afs32\ab \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\sl240\slmult1\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn\cf17\f4\fs32\b\dbch\af9\sl240\slmult1\sb240\sa120{\loch
14. Search app}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Launch tlo-search from the platform application folder. The Search app uses TLOHome/bootlist.csv for normal searches and TLOHome/setlists for Deep searches. Supply TLOHome with --TLOHome (or the retained hidden compatibility alias) or set the TLOHome environment variable. Search has no a developer-path fallback: if TLOHome is not supplied by one of those supported sources, Search stops with a clear error.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Use |AND| and |OR| to combine terms; parentheses and quoted phrases are supported.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Normal search treats an empty search box as all bootlist rows. Deep search requires a nonblank term and searches setlist text.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Select one or more results and choose View, or double-click one result. TLO derives the setlist filename directly from the Show value by calling the same postprocess filename creator used by inventory rather than scanning the setlists directory. Supported partial dates such as 1973-12-xx retain their internal date dashes in the filename.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Opening more than five setlists asks for confirmation. More than 30 setlists cannot be opened at once. Multiple text windows are cascaded down and right.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
On startup, Search uses --find when supplied; otherwise it reads clipboard text and immediately runs the first search. There is no preferences setting to disable or replace clipboard input. Old search_query and clipboard_prefill entries are ignored, and existing preferences files are preserved. The main Search box remains focused.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af12\afs32\ab \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\sl240\slmult1\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn\cf17\f4\fs32\b\dbch\af9\sl240\slmult1\sb240\sa120{\loch
15. Command-line quick reference}
