Non-destructive metadata and tag planning selected on the main Inventory window and inherited by Inventory, Tag, and Add Shows.}\cell\row\pard \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af12\afs32\ab \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\sl240\slmult1\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn\cf17\f4\fs32\b\dbch\af9\sl240\slmult1\sb240\sa120{\loch
19. GitHub update checks and Auto update}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Check for updates checks only the official approved production release, compares its build number to the installed build, and verifies independent signing metadata before trusting a newer package. On Windows, [general] update_downloads = msi is the default even when the current installation came from a ZIP. Setting update_downloads = zip explicitly chooses a matching portable ZIP; a missing INI or missing key defaults to MSI. On Linux, WSL and macOS, download the matching portable update ZIP.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
TLO checks update assets against independently signed metadata, including release identity, package/platform selection, declared size and SHA-256. It downloads to a temporary filename, rejects wrong or oversized content, and gives the verified download its final name without overwriting an unrelated existing Downloads file. A failed signature, digest or package validation aborts the download.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Auto update checks at startup at most once per day and asks before downloading a newer verified package. Choosing Skip does not install or download anything. Choosing Yes downloads the selected MSI or ZIP to Downloads. A Windows MSI can then be launched after confirmation; a ZIP must be applied manually. TLO does not automatically extract ZIP updates or delete the previous portable apps directory.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Only the matching signed release asset is selected. If the preferred Windows MSI or ZIP is unavailable, TLO does not silently select the other format. Select a different preference or download the desired official release asset manually.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Complete portable packages include applications, required TLO_DBs files (artists.sqlite, venues.txt, cities.sqlite and venues.sqlite), manuals, starter folders and a header-only bootlist.csv. Update packages exclude user-generated inventories, setlists and preferences. A database-inclusive update may replace only the managed TLO_DBs files; back up customized databases first.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Windows portable ZIP distributions have hybrid and onedir layouts. If manually installing a ZIP update, match its platform and layout. Do not overlay one layout onto another: replace apps/Windows as a complete unit and preserve its required runtime subfolders.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl276\slmult1\fi-259\li360\lin360\sb0\sa40\contextualspace{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Close TLO applications before installing any update. After a ZIP-to-MSI upgrade, verify the new installation before manually removing TLOHome/apps.}
\par \pard\plain \s58\loch\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa200\contextualspace\sl240\slmult1{\hich\af3\loch\f3\loch
Older portable updater versions may reject newer database-inclusive packages. Do not disable signature or manifest checks; use the official trusted manual installation path when an older updater cannot process a newer release.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af12\afs32\ab \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\sl240\slmult1\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn\cf17\f4\fs32\b\dbch\af9\sl240\slmult1\sb240\sa120{\loch\fs28\loch
20. Duplicate-folder cleanup utility - tlo-deleteDupes}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch\fs22\b0\loch
tlo-deleteDupes searches one fully qualified Input Path recursively for duplicate sibling folders. Candidate discovery combines exact copy families and same-artist/date matching. An exact copy family includes folders such as X, X (copy2), and X (copy3), so numbered copies are compared with one another even when X is absent or has different contents. TLO also compares same-parent folders when their normalized artist and date match even if the rest of the folder names differ.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch\fs22\b0\loch
Candidate folders are divided into content-equivalence clusters after TLO compares their complete recursive manifests. Copies are compared with one another as well as with unsuffixed folders. Each cluster of two or more identical trees keeps one folder and moves the other identical members. TLO prefers to keep a folder without a terminal (copyN) suffix. If more than one unsuffixed folder in the cluster is identical, the alphabetically first name is kept. If a cluster contains only numbered copies, the lowest-numbered copy is kept. Different name/size manifest clusters remain as separate variants. For example, if X differs from X (copy2) and X (copy3), but copy2 and copy3 match one another, TLO keeps X and copy2 and moves copy3.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch\fs22\b0\loch
Same-artist/date discovery recognizes a standalone four-digit 19xx/20xx year, yyyy-mm-dd style dates (including xx month/day components), and four-digit-year ranges. An unknown date without an actual four-digit year is not enough to broaden duplicate discovery. A matching artist and date is only a way to select folders for comparison; it does not by itself make a folder a duplicate.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Before any candidate folder is moved, TLO requires the same relative nested folder structure, including empty folders, and the same relative file names and file sizes as another member of its content-equivalence cluster. This cleanup utility deliberately does not compare file bytes or timestamps when deciding whether folders are duplicates. A folder whose manifest differs remains in its own content cluster rather than being discarded merely because another folder was previously chosen as a master. Note that recordings with identical file names and sizes but different audio are still treated as duplicates by this intentionally simple rule; inspect the duplicates holding area before deleting its contents.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch\fs22\b0\loch
When two folders in the same candidate component do not match, TLO appends one short CSV line to TLOHome/deleteDupesMismatches.txt. The three fields are folder name, folder name, simple reason. Examples include different number of files, different sub-structure, different file names, and 01.flac different sizes. Only folder names are recorded; full paths are not included. Folder names containing commas are automatically CSV-quoted. deletedDirs.txt remains only the list of folders actually moved.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
If the kept folder contains a FLAC with no stored MD5, duplicate cleanup preserves the entire cluster and reports the specific pathname and unset-MD5 reason. This is a missing integrity reference, not proof of corruption or a timeout; consider retaining a backup before any new checksum-bearing encoding. Before any duplicate member of a content-equivalence cluster is moved into the duplicates holding folder, TLO checks every FLAC file in that cluster's kept folder tree by fully decoding its audio stream with the bundled LGPL FFmpeg PCM validator and comparing decoded PCM MD5 and sample count with STREAMINFO. TLO explicitly treats the input as FLAC and allows only local file input, so renamed non-FLAC content is not accepted through ffmpeg content probing. This is a full audio-integrity check, not merely a FLAC header or tag read.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
Each full FLAC decode has a 180-second timeout. A missing/all-zero FLAC STREAMINFO MD5 means the encoder did not record a checksum; it is unverifiable, not corrupt, and cannot justify deletion or duplicate relocation. A timeout means the keeper is unverifiable, not healthy or corrupt. If any kept-folder FLAC times out, TLO reports the condition and leaves that entire duplicate cluster in place rather than relocating its other members.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
If a FLAC in the kept folder is confirmed corrupt, TLO looks for the same relative file in other members of that same content-equivalence cluster. Copy-suffixed repair sources are tried in numeric order: copy1, copy2, copy3, and so on. Qualifying unsuffixed repair sources are tried alphabetically. A candidate replacement must pass the full PCM/STREAMINFO verification, is staged separately, byte-verified before installation, and is decoded again after replacement. Before installation, TLO also creates a keeper backup and verifies that backup by size and SHA-256 against the pre-repair keeper. A partial or otherwise unverified backup is never used for rollback. If staging, backup creation, or atomic installation fails before installation completes, the original keeper bytes remain unchanged. If post-install verification fails, TLO rolls back only from the verified keeper backup.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch
If no healthy replacement is available for a confirmed-corrupt keeper FLAC, or if keeper validation is unverifiable because of timeout, I/O/resource, or validator-infrastructure failure, TLO leaves that duplicate cluster in place for later review. An unverifiable validation attempt is not treated as proof that the file is healthy or corrupt. Duplicate relocation proceeds only when the selected keeper is fully verified or successfully repaired.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch\fs22\b0\loch
TLO places qualifying duplicates in a folder named duplicates at the root of the partition containing the Input Path. TLO creates that folder when it does not already exist. On Linux/macOS, if the partition root is not writable, a writable sibling on the same filesystem is used instead; --duplicates-dir DIR allows an explicit same-filesystem destination. Cross-volume moves are never allowed. On Windows, E:\\Music therefore uses E:\\duplicates. On Linux/macOS, a search under /mnt/music uses /mnt/music/duplicates when /mnt/music is the filesystem mount root. TLO moves the entire qualifying duplicate folder tree in one same-partition directory move; it does not move child files individually. If an entry with the same name already exists in duplicates, TLO preserves both by adding a collision suffix such as (moved 2). If the search includes the partition root, TLO excludes the duplicates holding folder from further duplicate discovery. After a successful move, TLO appends the folder's full original pre-move path to TLOHome/deletedDirs.txt.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch\fs22\b0\loch
TLOHome is resolved using the same --TLOHome and TLOHome environment-variable logic used by the other TLO mains. The compatibility alias remains accepted but is intentionally omitted from user-facing help and documentation. The Input Path itself must be a fully qualified existing directory.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch\fs22\b0\loch
Example: tlo-deleteDupes.cmd --TLOHome C:\\TLOHome E:\\Music}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch\fs22\b0\loch
On Linux or macOS source execution: python3 tlo-deleteDupes.py --TLOHome /path/to/TLOHome /path/to/music}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch\fs22\b0\loch
Ctrl-C stops further work. The program closes deletedDirs.txt and deleteDupesMismatches.txt while unwinding and exits with cancellation status 130. Entries successfully logged before the interrupt remain in their respective log files; any folder already moved into the partition-root duplicates holding folder remains there.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60\rtlch\afs22\ab0 \ltrch\loch\fs22\b0\loch

\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af12\afs32\ab \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\sl240\slmult1\keep\sb480\sa0\keepn\cf17\f4\fs32\b\dbch\af9\pagebb\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa120{\loch\fs28\loch
21. Research log lookup - tlo-research}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch\fs22\b0\loch
Research provides a focused way to inspect the metadata and complete-path evidence already recorded in TLOHome/logs. The same search engine is used by the standalone tlo-research console application and by the Research button on the main Main GUI.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch\fs22\b0\loch
The input string is one of three forms: an artist followed by a date, a venue, or a date. Examples are Grateful Dead 1977-05-08, Barton Hall, 1977-05-08, and xxxx-xx-xx. Research uses the same canonical date parser as Inventory, so every Inventory-supported date representation is accepted, including numeric dates, textual-month dates, compact dates, slash dates, supported year ranges, same-month multi-day ranges such as 1970-04-12-13, 1970-04-12,13, and 12-13 April 1970, partial x-placeholder forms such as 2001-04-1x, 2004-0x-xx, 202x-xx-xx, 19xx-xx-xx, 20xx-xx-xx, and xxxx-xx-xx, plus a standalone four-digit 19xx/20xx year. A valid same-month multi-day range is normalized to its first day for matching; all days must be valid and strictly ascending. Research normalizes the date before matching. A date-only search matches the DATE field; artist plus date requires both ARTIST and DATE. Any other input is treated as a venue and is matched case-insensitively against VENUE. Research input is limited to 2,048 characters after whitespace normalization. Artist-plus-date classification examines at most the trailing 256 characters as the candidate date expression; longer Research input is rejected with a readable error.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch\fs22\b0\loch
Research reads every meta*.log in TLOHome/logs and returns the complete SHOW_NAME through END_SHOW_METADATA block for every matching record. It then uses MAIN_DIR_PATH and MUSIC_DIRS_JSON to locate every corresponding path line in comp*.log and displays those under COMP LOG. Research does not stop after the first metadata match. After the structured results, it performs a second exhaustive raw-line scan of every meta*.log and comp*.log and displays an ALL RELATED RAW LOG LINES section with every directly related occurrence, its log filename, and its line number. There is no per-file or per-query hit limit. This raw evidence is displayed even when there are zero structured metadata matches, which makes it possible to trace historical source strings or intermediate evidence that did not survive into the final metadata record.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch\fs22\b0\loch
In the Main GUI, click Research and type the research string in the input box. Search remains the active/default action whenever the Research dialog is active, so Return or keypad Enter anywhere in that dialog runs the search. Results open in a read-only, non-wrapping text window with vertical scrolling and a horizontal scrollbar along the bottom. Ctrl-A selects the complete result even when a button owns focus. The results window has a Search button, and Ctrl-F invokes the same action. The find dialog has a Search for: box and mutually exclusive Forward and Backwards choices, with Forward selected by default. Repeated searches continue in the chosen direction, ignore case, and wrap to the opposite end when there is no later/earlier match. The GUI uses the TLOHome already resolved when the Inventory app started.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch\fs22\b0\loch
In the CLI, output is written to the console. TLOHome can be supplied with --TLOHome; otherwise the TLOHome environment variable is used. The standalone app follows the same hidden compatibility precedence as the other TLO mains.}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch\fs22\b0\loch
Windows example: tlo-research.exe --TLOHome C:\\TLOHome "Grateful Dead 1977-05-08"}
\par \pard\plain \s0\rtlch\af12\afs20\alang1033 \ltrch\lang1033\langfe1033\hich\af2\loch\sl276\slmult1\ql\widctlpar\hyphpar0\sb0\sa200\ltrpar\cf0\f2\fs20\lang1033\kerning0\dbch\af10\langfe1033\sl240\slmult1\sb0\sa60{\loch\fs22\b0\loch
Windows venue example: tlo-research.exe --TLOHome C:\\TLOHome "Barton Hall"}
